<p>Google has changed parts of Search in Europe to comply with the EU Digital Markets Act. The changes affect travel and other vertical-search experiences, and Google says they may reduce the visibility and detail available to users. For SEO teams, the important point is that the search page itself can change even when the site's own pages do not.</p>
<figure class="article-image"><img src="/assets/images/blog/google-regional-search-experience-what-seos-need-to-check-2026.svg" alt="European Google Search layout with aggregator, supplier and organic result sections"></figure>
<h2>What changed in European Search</h2><p>Google says its EEA changes give more visibility to eligible vertical search services in areas such as travel. Reporting from Reuters describes a redesigned layout with prominent aggregator units and less real-time detail in some result areas.</p>
<h2>Why this matters for SEO</h2><p>Rankings are only one part of search visibility. If a page appears in a different result module, receives less visual detail or sits lower on the page, click behavior can change without a classic ranking penalty.</p>
<h2>What travel and local businesses should monitor</h2><p>Track impressions, clicks, CTR and conversions separately for European markets. Compare brand, non-brand, local and transactional queries. Also record changes in the SERP features that appear for your highest-value query groups.</p>
<h2>How to avoid bad conclusions</h2><p>Do not compare global traffic with European traffic and call the difference an algorithm update. Segment by country, query type, device and landing-page group first. Then compare the dates when Google's new layout was introduced with your own analytics data.</p>
<h2>What to improve on the site</h2><ul><li>Keep business facts clear and crawlable.</li><li>Use structured data where it genuinely describes the page.</li><li>Maintain accurate prices, availability and business information where applicable.</li><li>Build demand beyond one Google result feature.</li></ul>
<h2>How international SEO teams should report the change</h2><p>Create a separate Europe Search change note in the SEO log. Record the affected countries, query sets, observed SERP changes and traffic impact. This keeps a regulatory SERP change separate from confirmed ranking updates.</p>
<h2>Related ToolBoxKart guides</h2><p>Read <a href="/blog/google-regional-search-experience-what-seos-need-to-check">the regional Search guide</a>, <a href="/blog/optimize-pages-for-google-ai-search">how to optimize for Google AI Search</a>, <a href="/blog/google-ai-search-visibility-report">the AI Search visibility report guide</a>, and <a href="/blog/search-console-ai-performance-report-guide">the Search Console AI report guide</a>.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Does this affect all Google users?</h3><p>The reported DMA changes are focused on users in the European Economic Area.</p><h3>Is this a ranking algorithm update?</h3><p>No. It is a change to the Search experience tied to regulatory compliance.</p><h3>What should SEOs measure?</h3><p>Measure country-level impressions, clicks, CTR, conversions and SERP feature changes for important query groups.</p>
